Chapter: Fat32ImgLongname

  cites Foreword chapter Fat32
  cites Foreword chapter Fat16
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 The guest reading a FAT32 image the HOST wrote, which is the only arm that can
 tell the two VFAT implementations apart on this width. `build-img.ps1` writes
 long-name runs in PowerShell; `Fat32.codex` reads them in Codex. A round trip
 through either one alone would pass while proving nothing about the other,
 which is the shape `BrotliBeatsOpus` was written about and the shape
 `codex/test/img-longname` already covers for FAT16.

 Nothing covered it for FAT32. `fat32-find-in-dir` learned to assemble runs on
 2026-09-01 and `fat32-add-dir-entry` learned to write them the next day, and
 every grader either laid its runs down by hand or read back what Codex itself
 had just written. Both are the encoder agreeing with itself.

 The fixture is the same six name shapes `codex/test/img-longname` chose,
 because they are the ones that discriminate rather than the ones that are
 realistic:

   X86_64CodeGen.codex   19 characters, two records. The name the old flat SRC/
                         layout mangled to X86_64CO.COD.
   readme.codex          differs from its own 8.3 form only in CASE, so it
                         still needs a run; a writer comparing case-blindly
                         would store README.COD and the arm below catches it.
   Thirtee.codex         thirteen characters: exactly one record, no
                         terminator.
   Fourteen.codex        fourteen: exactly two, the second holding one unit.
   CollideOne.codex      these two share their first six characters, so their
   CollideTwo.codex      aliases must differ by the number after the tilde.
                         Their CONTENTS differ, so an arm reading one and
                         getting the other's bytes is a failure this can
                         express rather than a silent agreement.

 Any name ending in `.codex` needs a run whatever its base, because the format
 stores three extension characters and `codex` is five.

 THE ALIAS ARM IS THE ONE THAT WOULD SURVIVE A BROKEN READER, so it is stated
 rather than left implied: `SRC/X86_64CO.COD` is what the old mangling
 produced and it must NOT resolve, because the host writes no such entry. An
 arm that only asked for the long names would pass against a volume carrying
 both.

 REGENERATING THIS FIXTURE, because the FAT16 one recorded no command and is
 therefore not reproducible. From the repository root, with the six files above
 in a scratch directory holding no trailing newline:

   build/build-img.ps1 -PeInput build/boot/blockladder.efi
     -Out codex/test/fat32-img-longname.disk -SourceDir <scratch>
     -TotalSectors 79000 -Fat32

 79000 is not arbitrary. The ESP is what carries the volume and it is
 `TotalSectors * 7/8 - 2081` after the fact store takes an eighth off the top,
 and a REAL FAT32 volume needs at least 65525 clusters or firmware classifies
 it as something else. 79000 sectors yields 65964, and the run above prints
 both numbers. `blockladder.efi` is named because it is tracked and stable; its
 bytes are never read by this chapter.

 We say:

Section: Reporting

  f32il-say : Maybe Text -> Text
  f32il-say (m) = when m
    is Just (t) -> t
    is None -> "<none>"

  f32il-found : Maybe Fat16DirEntry -> Text
  f32il-found (m) = when m
    is Just (e) -> "found " & (e.de-name)
    is None -> "<none>"

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console, Device.Block] Nothing
  opening = act
    vol <- fat32-init 2048
    print-line-uni ("root cluster " & show (vol.f32-root-cluster)
      & " spc " & show (vol.f32-sectors-per-cluster))

    a <- fat32-read-text vol "SRC/X86_64CodeGen.codex"
    print-line-uni ("nineteen characters " & f32il-say a)

    b <- fat32-read-text vol "SRC/readme.codex"
    print-line-uni ("case only " & f32il-say b)

    c <- fat32-read-text vol "SRC/Thirtee.codex"
    print-line-uni ("thirteen " & f32il-say c)

    d <- fat32-read-text vol "SRC/Fourteen.codex"
    print-line-uni ("fourteen " & f32il-say d)

    e <- fat32-read-text vol "SRC/CollideOne.codex"
    print-line-uni ("collide one " & f32il-say e)

    f <- fat32-read-text vol "SRC/CollideTwo.codex"
    print-line-uni ("collide two " & f32il-say f)

    g <- fat32-read-text vol "SRC/X86_64CO.COD"
    print-line-uni ("old mangling absent " & f32il-say g)

    h <- fat32-read-text vol "SRC/NoSuchChapter.codex"
    print-line-uni ("absent name " & f32il-say h)

    i <- fat32-resolve-path vol "SRC"
    print-line-uni ("SRC itself " & f32il-found i)
  end

